  "theorem_name": "UniformlyBoundedDenseCoreQuadraticConvergenceExtendsEverywhere",
  "declared_proposition": "Let q_n be continuous Hermitian quadratic forms on a complex Hilbert space H, with associated forms B_n, and let D be dense in H. If sup_n ||B_n||<infinity and q_n(x) converges for every x in D, then B_n(x,y) and q_n(x) converge for every x,y in H to a bounded Hermitian form. Positivity passes to the limit. Positivity and monotonicity on D do not replace the uniform bound: q_n(x)=sum_(k<=n) k|x_k|^2 has all those core properties on c_00 while ||B_n||=n and diverges at an explicit l^2 vector.",
  "mathematical_argument": "Polarization gives convergence of B_n(u,v) for u,v in D. If ||B_n||<=M and u,v approximate x,y, then the Cauchy difference B_n-B_m away from D is bounded by 2M(||x-u||||y||+||u||||y-v||). First choose u,v, then use core convergence. This proves convergence everywhere and a norm-M limit. For the no-go, positive diagonal sections sum_(k<=n) k|x_k|^2 increase and stabilize on every c_00 vector. The l^2 vector with |x_(2^j)|^2=2^(-j) has norm one but q_(2^J)(x)=J.",
  "reproducible_computation": {
    "theorem": "Let q_n be continuous Hermitian quadratic forms on a complex Hilbert space H, with associated forms B_n, and let D be dense in H. If sup_n ||B_n||<infinity and q_n(x) converges for every x in D, then B_n(x,y) and q_n(x) converge for every x,y in H to a bounded Hermitian form. Positivity passes to the limit. Positivity and monotonicity on D do not replace the uniform bound: q_n(x)=sum_(k<=n) k|x_k|^2 has all those core properties on c_00 while ||B_n||=n and diverges at an explicit l^2 vector.",
    "proof": "Polarization gives convergence of B_n(u,v) for u,v in D. If ||B_n||<=M and u,v approximate x,y, then the Cauchy difference B_n-B_m away from D is bounded by 2M(||x-u||||y||+||u||||y-v||). First choose u,v, then use core convergence. This proves convergence everywhere and a norm-M limit. For the no-go, positive diagonal sections sum_(k<=n) k|x_k|^2 increase and stabilize on every c_00 vector. The l^2 vector with |x_(2^j)|^2=2^(-j) has norm one but q_(2^J)(x)=J.",
